{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"QUESTION 2 -- will renaming SC->AC cause conflicts?\n\nFound three, one expensive:\n1. regver rotation. criteria_routing.json's check strings are hashed into registry_version(), which is bound into every signed manifest. Empirically confirmed a one-phrase edit rotates it: b058e30f0de6a907 -> 23913c1f6987711b. That read as stale-regver at the claim gate, invalidating attestations on 632 tickets (~40 open/in_progress).\n2. Believed at the time to be a semantic merge, not a rename.\n3. Epic clarity +2 becomes automatic since AC is already a universal floor.\n\nCORRECTION made in the next turn: point 2 was wrong. Based it on the guide's PROSE. The criteria themselves pool the two everywhere -- F1 opens 'each acceptance/success criterion' with applies_at {suppress_types:[bug]} (no scope restriction, so containers too), G3 'each parent acceptance/success criterion', G7 fetches both as one contract. No criterion applies a different bar to SC. Altitude is carried by ROUTING (G3 applies_at scope:[container], firing on has-children), not by heading name. So the split is vestigial vocabulary and the guide prose is the actual defect.\n\nMerging is also strictly TIGHTER: _count_ac_items counts '- [ ]' only under '## Acceptance Criteria', so SC criteria never reach the P1 floor and can never carry [operator-attested] (the verifier matches that token only on checkbox text)."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1784950165339477001,"uuid":"726df2d7-5d04-4b87-8fee-0cf9be47c89f"}